Liquid AI's new d1-ultra answered {{d1-ultra.banking77.accuracy|pct1}} of BANKING77 and {{d1-ultra.clinc.accuracy|pct1}} of CLINC150+OOS right. Liquid's earlier d1 answered {{d1.banking77.accuracy|pct1}} and {{d1.clinc.accuracy|pct1}}. We ran both on {{d1-ultra.banking77.t_first|date_dmy}}, the day after Liquid AI's launch post for d1-ultra, through decide() in NeuroLink, Juspay's AI harness.

Same machine, same benchmark kit, the two models side by side, each one request at a time. Every request is on tape, with every response that came back, and the API key is masked in the recording before it is written. Every number below was recomputed from that recording and the benchmarks' public answer key by two separate programs, one in Node and one in Python, that have to agree byte for byte.

BANKING77 ({{d1-ultra.banking77.rows|int}} banking requests)
- d1-ultra: {{d1-ultra.banking77.accuracy|pct1}} right ({{d1-ultra.banking77.correct|int}})
- d1: {{d1.banking77.accuracy|pct1}} right ({{d1.banking77.correct|int}})

CLINC150+OOS ({{d1-ultra.clinc.rows|int}} requests, {{d1-ultra.clinc.oos_rows|int}} of them fit none of the listed intents)
- d1-ultra: {{d1-ultra.clinc.accuracy|pct1}} right. It said "out of scope" to {{d1-ultra.clinc.recall|pct1}} of the requests that were, and {{d1-ultra.clinc.false_alarms|int}} times to requests that had an intent.
- d1: {{d1.clinc.accuracy|pct1}} right, {{d1.clinc.recall|pct1}} of the out-of-scope requests caught, {{d1.clinc.false_alarms|int}} false alarms.
- On the requests that did have an intent, d1 was ahead: {{d1.clinc.in_scope_accuracy|pct1}} against {{d1-ultra.clinc.in_scope_accuracy|pct1}}. d1-ultra's lead on this benchmark comes from catching more out-of-scope requests.

THE TAPE
